One particularly strong opening:
For a stage presentation, I would actually open with this before defining SOLID:

          “The first version of a software system is usually not what makes it complex. 
            Complexity emerges from the accumulation of change. SOLID gives us a vocabulary 
            and a set of design principles for controlling how that change propagates through the system.”

That sounds considerably more like a senior architect's perspective than starting with “SOLID stands for…”.

And one strong closing line:

        “Good design is not the absence of change; it is the ability of a system to absorb change 
        without making every part of the system participate in it.”

***************************************
When I talk about SOLID principles, I don't look at them as five independent rules that we apply mechanically while writing classes.
I see SOLID as a set of design principles that help us manage one of the fundamental challenges in software engineering:

      **Change.**

Because software systems are rarely difficult because of the first version we build. 
They become difficult when the business evolves, requirements change, new integrations are introduced, 
traffic increases, teams grow, and previously independent components start becoming tightly coupled.

So, from my perspective, the real question behind SOLID is not:
            **"How do I write a SOLID class?"**

  The more important question is:
            **"How do I design my system so that a change in one area does not unnecessarily propagate through unrelated areas?"**

That perspective gives us a much deeper understanding of SOLID.
Let's start with the **Single Responsibility Principle**.

SRP is commonly described as:

**"A class should have one reason to change."**

I think the phrase "reason to change" is much more important than the idea of having only one responsibility or one method.

Consider an OrderService responsible for order validation, persistence, email notifications, invoice generation and perhaps payment orchestration.

Technically, this class may still work perfectly.

The problem is that these responsibilities are driven by different forces.

The database may change independently of the notification mechanism.

The notification mechanism may change independently of invoice generation.

And business rules may change independently of all of them.

So the problem isn't simply that the class is large.

The deeper problem is that **multiple independent axes of change have been coupled together.**

As a developer, SRP helps me create code that is easier to understand, test and modify.

As an architect, I use SRP to identify **cohesive boundaries around independently changing responsibilities**.

And that distinction is important because SRP does not mean that every class should contain only a few lines of code.

Over-applying SRP can actually make a system worse by creating excessive fragmentation and unnecessary abstractions.

The objective is not smaller classes.

The objective is **high cohesion and controlled change.**

The second principle is the **Open/Closed Principle**.

It states that software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

A common example is a payment system.

Suppose a PaymentService contains conditional logic for credit cards, UPI, PayPal and several other payment methods.

Initially, this may look simple.

But every new payment method requires us to modify the same piece of business logic.

As the number of variations increases, the probability of regression also increases.

We can instead introduce a PaymentProcessor abstraction and allow different payment mechanisms to provide their own implementations.

Now the core payment orchestration can remain relatively stable while new payment capabilities are introduced through extension.

But there is an important architectural nuance here.

OCP does not mean that existing code must never be modified.

That would be unrealistic.

The real principle is about **strategic stability**.

We should identify the parts of the system that are relatively stable and isolate the dimensions that are expected to vary.

So rather than asking:

**"Where can I introduce an interface?"**

I ask:

**"What is likely to change independently, and can I isolate that variation behind a stable abstraction?"**

That is a much more practical interpretation of OCP.

The third principle is the **Liskov Substitution Principle**.

This principle is particularly interesting because it moves beyond structural design into **behavioral contracts**.

The idea is that if a type claims to be a substitute for another type, clients should be able to use that subtype without violating the assumptions established by the abstraction.

A common example is an abstraction such as Bird with a fly operation, followed by introducing Penguin as a Bird that cannot fly.

The problem is not really the Penguin.

The problem is that our abstraction has encoded an incorrect assumption about the domain.

This is why LSP is not simply about inheritance.

It is about whether an abstraction represents a valid behavioral contract.

An abstraction communicates expectations about things such as valid inputs, outputs, invariants and supported behavior.

If every client needs special-case logic such as:

"if this is a particular subtype, don't call this method",

then the abstraction itself may be telling us that our model is incorrect.

So from an architect's perspective, LSP is fundamentally about **designing trustworthy abstractions**.

The fourth principle is the **Interface Segregation Principle**.

It says that clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.

Imagine a large enterprise interface containing employee management, payroll, leave management, benefits and reporting operations.

Different consumers may need completely different capabilities.

If every consumer depends on the same large contract, then a change in one capability can unnecessarily affect unrelated consumers.

Interface segregation allows us to define contracts around actual client needs.

This reduces coupling and improves testability and evolvability.

And again, I would avoid interpreting ISP as:

**"Create as many small interfaces as possible."**

That would simply replace one form of complexity with another.

The architectural question is:

**"Do these clients have different responsibilities, different capabilities, or different reasons to change?"**

If they do, separate contracts may provide meaningful value.

If they don't, splitting the interface may simply introduce ceremony without improving the design.

Finally, we come to the **Dependency Inversion Principle**.

For me, this is one of the most important principles from an architectural perspective.

The principle says that high-level policy should not depend directly on low-level implementation details.

Both should depend on abstractions.

Consider an OrderService that directly instantiates a MySQL repository.

Now the business logic is coupled to a specific infrastructure implementation.

If we introduce an abstraction such as OrderRepository, the application layer can express what it needs without knowing how persistence is implemented.

Infrastructure can then provide the appropriate implementation.

This gives us a dependency structure where the details depend on the abstractions required by the higher-level policy, rather than the business logic being dictated by infrastructure.

And there is an important distinction here:

**Dependency Injection is not the same thing as Dependency Inversion.**

Dependency Injection is a mechanism or technique for supplying dependencies.

Dependency Inversion is an architectural principle concerned with the direction and ownership of dependencies.

A framework such as Spring can help us implement dependency injection, but simply using @Autowired does not automatically mean that the architecture follows DIP.

The real question is:

**"Which layer owns the abstraction, and in which direction do the dependencies flow?"**

Now, when I step back and look at all five principles together, I don't see five isolated rules.

I see a coherent approach to managing software complexity.

**SRP** helps us establish meaningful responsibility boundaries.

**OCP** helps us isolate expected variation and protect stable parts of the system.

**LSP** helps us ensure that our abstractions preserve behavioral contracts.

**ISP** helps us design focused contracts around actual client needs.

And **DIP** helps us control dependency direction and prevent business policy from becoming coupled to infrastructure details.

Together, they influence some of the most important properties of a production system:

**cohesion, coupling, testability, extensibility, maintainability and changeability.**

And there is one final point that I consider particularly important.

SOLID should not become a checklist.

We should not look at a class and say:

"Do I have an interface?"

"Do I have dependency injection?"

"Do I have five small classes?"

That can actually lead to over-engineering.

Instead, I prefer to start with the system's **business requirements and its expected sources of change**.

I ask:

What is stable?

What is volatile?

What changes together?

What needs to evolve independently?

Where are my dependency boundaries?

And which abstractions actually provide architectural value?

So, ultimately, I would summarize SOLID this way:

**SOLID is not about writing more abstractions.**

**It is about creating the right abstractions and boundaries so that change remains localized, dependencies remain intentional, and the system can evolve without continuously increasing its complexity.**

And that, for me, is the real value of SOLID in production software architecture.
